CRONTAB(5)                     P32W Unix                      CRONTAB(5)

NAME
       crontab - table of commands for cron

DESCRIPTION
       /etc/crontab tells cron(8) what to run and when. Each line
       has five time fields and a command, separated by blanks
       (spaces or tabs):

              minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week command

       minute         0-59
       hour           0-23
       day-of-month   1-31
       month          1-12
       day-of-week    0-6, 0 = Sunday

       Each field is a list of items separated by commas (no
       blanks), and each item is one of

       *       every value of the field
       n       the value n
       a-b     the values a to b
       */n, a-b/n
               every n-th value of the field, or of a to b,
               counting from its start

       A line runs when all five fields match the current minute,
       in local time (TZ or /etc/TZ; see tz(5)). The command is the
       rest of the line; it is run by sh -c, as root, in /, with
       its input and output on /dev/null unless it redirects them.

       Lines that are empty or start with # are skipped, as are
       lines with fewer than five fields or no command.

EXAMPLES
       0 3 * * *       /bin/sync
       */15 * * * *    date >> /tmp/clock.log
       30 8 * * 1-5    echo weekday >> /usr/ken/log
       0 0 1,15 * *    rm -f /tmp/clock.log

FILES
       /etc/crontab

SEE ALSO
       cron(8), at(1), sh(1), tz(5)

BUGS
       There are no month or day names (jan, mon), no 7 for Sunday,
       and no @reboot or @daily. When both day-of-month and
       day-of-week are restricted, both must match (a POSIX cron
       runs the line when either does). Bad fields are not
       reported; text where a number belongs counts as 0. A line
       is read into 256 bytes; a longer one is split.
